# This repository's own compose graph, measured at two scopes. One row per scope: a NULL scope is
# the graph taken whole, and a scope is one directory's cut of it. Each row counts the graph's
# nodes, edges and components, and the two figures those counts give.
#
# Beside `rank/graph_measures.sqlc`, on purpose
#   That relation measures the graphs the filings make; this measures the graph this repository's
#   queries make. It carries `rank/compose_edges.sqlc`'s point one step further: two edge lists of
#   one shape in one directory claim the two graphs are alike, and two measuring relations make them
#   comparable. Without this file the compose graph would have no row anywhere, and every figure
#   about it would live in one program.
#
# Read from the files, and outside `pm`
#   `public.compose_edge` comes from `assets/sqlc/` rather than from a filed document, so this
#   relation may say something about this repository and must never join a filing to say something
#   about a subject. That is the line `rank/compose_edges.sqlc` states, and this file keeps to it.
#
# A template is in one directory, where a layer can be under two filings
#   `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` cuts by filing, and a node may appear under two filings, so the
#   per-filing node counts sum to more than the whole graph's. A template lives in exactly one
#   directory, so this cut splits the nodes, and `sum(n_nodes)` equals the whole graph's node count
#   exactly. Two different kinds of cut: a reader who compares the two relations' sums without
#   noticing will conclude that one of them has lost nodes.
#
# Why a directory is the cut worth taking
#   It is the only grouping of these nodes anybody keeps by hand, and the one a reader finds their
#   way by. So the question it answers is whether the directory tree is where composition lives,
#   and `rank/compose_decomposition.sqlc` states the answer.
#
# The directory is the top-level one, as `assets/sqlc/README.md` uses the word
#   `queries/observations/16-guard-reach.sqlc` is in `queries`, not in `queries/observations`. That
#   is the grouping the README's own directory list shows and the one a reader goes by, and it is
#   the coarser of the two, so a cut at the finer level can only lose more. Cutting at every level
#   would be a different relation and a different question.
#
# A template in no directory gets a scope of its own
#   A few templates sit at the root of `assets/sqlc/`, so `'(root)'` is a scope like any other.
#   Dropping them would leave nodes in no scope, and the sum would stop being a check.
#
# Loops without direction, and only those
#   The two figures are about the graph with the direction of its edges ignored, so the walk adds
#   each edge both ways before counting, exactly as `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` does. Followed in the
#   direction of composition, the compose graph has no loop at all, which is a different question
#   and a different sense of the word: `composition/descent.sqlc`'s header sets out all four.
#
# Probe
#   Move one template into another directory and recompose: the whole-graph row must not move at
#   all, and the summed loops must. A change that moves both is reading the wrong scope.
-- rank/compose_edges.sqlc, walked for components both ways, counted whole and by directory.
WITH RECURSIVE
edge AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT e.parent AS a, e.child AS b
    FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc) ) e
),
node AS (
    SELECT a AS name FROM edge UNION SELECT b FROM edge
),
placed AS (
    SELECT n.name,
           CASE WHEN position('/' IN n.name) > 0 THEN split_part(n.name, '/', 1) ELSE '(root)' END
             AS directory
    FROM node n
),
scoped_node AS (
    SELECT NULL::text AS scope, p.name FROM placed p
  UNION ALL
    SELECT p.directory, p.name FROM placed p
),
scoped_edge AS (
    SELECT NULL::text AS scope, e.a, e.b FROM edge e
  UNION ALL
    SELECT pa.directory, e.a, e.b
    FROM edge e
    JOIN placed pa ON pa.name = e.a
    JOIN placed pb ON pb.name = e.b AND pb.directory = pa.directory
),
sym AS (
    SELECT scope, a, b FROM scoped_edge
  UNION
    SELECT scope, b, a FROM scoped_edge
),
reach(scope, root, at) AS (
      SELECT s.scope, s.name, s.name FROM scoped_node s
    UNION
      SELECT r.scope, r.root, y.b
      FROM reach r JOIN sym y ON y.scope IS NOT DISTINCT FROM r.scope AND y.a = r.at
),
comp AS (SELECT scope, root, min(at) AS component FROM reach GROUP BY scope, root)
SELECT n.scope,
       count(DISTINCT n.name)                                              AS n_nodes,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM scoped_edge w
         WHERE w.scope IS NOT DISTINCT FROM n.scope)                       AS m_edges,
       count(DISTINCT c.component)                                         AS c_components,
       count(DISTINCT n.name) - count(DISTINCT c.component)                AS rank_of_incidence,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM scoped_edge w
         WHERE w.scope IS NOT DISTINCT FROM n.scope)
         - count(DISTINCT n.name) + count(DISTINCT c.component)            AS cycle_space_dim
FROM      scoped_node n
JOIN      comp c ON c.scope IS NOT DISTINCT FROM n.scope AND c.root = n.name
GROUP BY  n.scope
